 long as you can kind of  get a you know very easy to  uh use a kind of registration process  you know maybe some look and feel tweaks  uh and some good documentation up and  running start with the basics start from  there and  kind of iterate over time uh is the  definitely the way that i would go  uh you know one thing that uh you know  ideally you would want to avoid is kind  of rolling out the apis and some sort of  mechanism  you know god forbid it being some sort  of like you know 300 page api  you know document or something like that  that you have to email over to all your  your app uh your app developers uh you  know  the quick more quickly can roll out  developer portal uh the better  in my mind but that was a great question  all right uh let's see you mentioned  capturing feedback from consumers uh  what are some examples of how that can  be done  once again another great question i'm  gonna go back to developer portal here  a lot of times when it comes to feedback  uh you know it could be done through  forms  if you have uh this this idea of  implementing a form  that could be one mechanism um you know  on the developer portal itself there  could be a feedback link  we talked about the role of the api  evangelist or maybe a product owner or  someone who can you know maybe  proactively reach out to application  developers and and kind of get feedback  there  the the importance here is you know not  only do you want to capture feedback and  there's a lot of different ways as i  just described of doing that  but you want to make sure that there is  a process that you build into your  api program and your you know software  delivery life cycle  of how do we take that feedback and  incorporate it into the next iteration  of the api  i think that's the trick here i think  there's a lot of times when people go  down this approach  they think about how to capture the  feedback but they don't really think  about how to  act upon it right so when you're  starting to think about  um you know things like for example we  promote uh like an agile  type you know methodology when it comes  to api development  the reason we promote that is because  you know with with something like agile  and iterative approach  development you can very quickly kind of  turn on a dime and implement  uh you know new feature requests or you  know new feedback you're getting  you know much more quickly than if you  were to do something like in a waterfall  type  type delivery model so it's sort of all  related to that you just want to make  sure that there's a  good mechanism of getting that feedback  back to your your product owners and  your api developers just so that they  can act upon it  that's another great question as well  uh so given the amount of time we have  left uh this is probably the last  question i'll answer  live uh and then we will kind of maybe  jump into answering the rest of the  questions via chat  let's see it's the developer portal for  external users only okay that's a great  question  in my mind and this is what i tell my  customers well  you your developer portal should not  differentiate between internal and  external  and what i mean by that is and this this  kind of goes into the whole philosophy  of your api design as well you know you  don't want to design an api for  oh these apis are for external consumers  and i'm going to design  my internal apis differently you know  from the end of the day you want to look  at your api  consumers as sort of one entity because  at the end of the day  everyone whether they be internal or  external application developers within  your organization  wants apis that are easy to use they  want to get access to those apis  in a quick and efficient manner so you  should always approach it  and always try to strive to make your  apis  as easy to consume as possible  regardless of if there are you know an  external partner a person working  building an application in their garage  or the person just sitting on the  opposite side of the building  right uh you know you have much more  flexibility of course if there are  internal applications because maybe  those application developers already  understand the back-end  services and and what that you know  pertains but you don't want to go down  the path of building kind of  two different versions of the same api  one for internal one for external  if you have the option of building one  api that both parties can use  you know it from a maintainability it's  a lot easier but also just  uh in terms of happy app developers um  you know once again putting you  yourself in the shoes of what can i do  to make their lives a lot easier  so that they can go build their  applications much quicker that is sort  of the approach you want to take so  developer portal falls in that same line  as well to kind of directly answer your  question  when you're building on a developer  portal a lot of times we we tell our  customers that they should build one  developer portal  now what the person can see in that  developer portal in terms of like what  avail  apis are available to them that's where  something like role-based access control  will come in  as an external person using the  developer portal you know i may not see  all the apis that an internal user would  see  but it should still be one portal you  you i  ideally you won't want you won't want to  get into a scenario where you have to  maintain and upkeep two separate  developer portals just from a  cost and operations uh perspective  all right so with that uh i think that  is a wrap for the webcast so once again  thank you so much for for kind of  spending the time  today with me to kind of go over this  topic once again you know it's something  that  we see a lot with our customers it's  sort of the ongoing message that we  promote a lot with our  uh customer base so i really hope that  you know you found this useful  i hope that there is um you know some of  the questions we brought up you know  some of the topics that we talked about  will get you kind of thinking about you  know where you're at with your api  program and more importantly kind of how  do you want to get to that  ideal state of having this whole you  know user-centric approach to your apis  and once again if there are you know any  questions you have definitely reach out  uh you know we have a very robust kind  of community to kind of answer your  questions  once again i mentioned before we have a  very uh  great and robust documentation site for  apogee that not only goes through all  the apogee capabilities but also  even concepts like restful api design  versioning and all these different  things developer portal for example  um so i highly recommend you guys uh  take a look at that um  if if you know that interests you so so  that's a wrap on the webcast once again  thanks again and i hope you guys have a  great day  you
 

